### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

compiler

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When an element is the only root node of an `@if`, `@for` or `@switch` branch, Angular uses its tag name and attributes to pick the `<ng-content select>` slot it goes into. That works for HTML elements, but not for SVG or MathML elements when the slot uses a tag selector: they always end up in the default slot.

I ran into it with a button that has a dedicated icon slot:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'ui-button',
  template: `
    <button type="button">
      <span class="icon"><ng-content select="svg, img" /></span>
      <span class="label"><ng-content /></span>
    </button>
  `,
})
export class UiButton {}
```

```html
<!-- works: the svg goes into .icon -->
<ui-button>
  <svg>...</svg>
  Save
</ui-button>

<!-- broken: the svg goes into .label -->
<ui-button>
  @if (saving()) {
    <svg class="spinner">...</svg>
  } @else {
    <svg>...</svg>
  }
  Save
</ui-button>
```

The same template with `<img>` instead of `<svg>` in both branches is projected into `.icon` as expected. Attribute and class selectors are not affected, only tag selectors. I see the same behavior with `@else if`, `@for`, `@empty`, `@switch` cases, `<svg *ngIf>` as the root node, and `<math>` elements.

The generated code shows why. The control flow template gets the tag name with its namespace prefix:

```js
ɵɵconditionalCreate(35, App_Conditional_35_Template, 2, 0, ":svg:svg", 10)(36, App_Conditional_36_Template, 2, 0, ":svg:svg", 7);
```

so at runtime the `svg` selector is compared with `:svg:svg` and doesn't match. The name comes from `ingestControlFlowInsertionPoint` in `packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/ingest.ts`, which returns `root.name` (or `root.tagName`) without stripping the prefix. Regular elements and structural templates already go through `splitNsName`, so I think the fix is to do the same there. This has been the case since control flow blocks started taking part in content projection in v17.

Adding `ngProjectAs="svg"` to the `<svg>` works around it.

I'll send a PR with a fix and tests.

